=TITLE Why Linux?

=slide
=head1 Why Linux?

Community Education

Tom Browder [tom.browder@gmail.com]

Raku Computer Language Core Developer

L<https://Raku.org>

2026-10-09

=slide
=head1 Background

=item Retired from USAF in 1987 and ManTech in 2017

=item Have had a passion for computers since my days as a card-punching Algol- and Fortran-programming cadet at the U.S. Air Force Academy.

=item After retirement from the USAF, I earned an MS in Engineering Mechanics from Clemson University, and have been employed otherwise as a vulnerability analyst, program manager, computer network system administrator for mixed Windows and Unix/Linux networks, and software engineer.

=slide
=head1 Background

=item My interests include Raku (formerly known as Perl 6), PostScript, PDF, and C++ programming, and the whole world of Linux and Free and Open Source Freeware in General.

=item I am not a windows expert, but I am pretty computer savvy.

=item Most of what I will say is opinionated, but based on many years of professional experience in the digital world.

=slide
=head1 Hypothesis

=item Young people interested in careers involved with science, mathematics, engineering, art, music, or almost any technical field should be introduced to Linux.

=item Computers running Linux come loaded with powerful Free and Open Source Software (FOSS).

=item If a Linux system is not available, they should still be introduced to FOSS on Windows or Mac computers.

=slide
=head1 What is Linux?

=item More formally known as GNU/Linux, it’s a free operating system (OS) invented by Linus Torvald to provide a UNIX-like system with all the standard command-line and graphics tools that accompany such a system.

=item Linux, rather than Windows or Mac, provides much of the behind-the-scenes cloud computing in data centers and high-performance computing facilities. It (or forks of it) also powers the Android, the Chromebook, and much of the Internet of Things.

=slide
=head1 What is Linux? (cont.)

=item For a much more detailed look at Linux compared to other OSs, see this link: L<https://www.lifewire.com/operating-systems-2625912>

=slide
=head1 Why Linux?

=item The commmand-line environment (equivalent to the Power Shell window on Windows) provides the user with almost complete freedom to automate tasks typical in research or other work in technical fields.

=item Linux also gives the user much greater control over what happens and doesn’t happen on his or her computer.

=item As a bonus, Linux comes with thousands of free applications in categories like office suites, publishing, graphics, CAD, FEA, education, science, mathematics, music, art, and many others,

=slide
=head1 Linux vs. Windows: Windows

Windows:

=item Is everywhere

=item Is primarily a GUI operation

=item Is expensive

=item Hides internals

=item Has a non-case-sensitive file system

=item Updates are often awkward and difficult to control

=slide
=head1 Linux vs. Windows: Linux

Linux:

=item Is the opposite of Windows in most respects

=item Is everywhere, although it is usually working behind the scenes

=item Is FREE

=item Shows as much or as little of the internals as you want to see

=item Has a case-sensitive file system

=item Performs upgrades only if you want it to

=item Is unparalleled for the scientist or engineer: a powerful, “programmable calculator"

=slide
=head1 Linux vs. Windows: Other views

Other opinions:

=item L<https://www.popsci.com/switch-to-linux-operating-system>

